[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Core Leadership Styles and When to Apply Them"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Effective technical leadership requires adaptability. Different situations, team maturity levels, and project phases demand different approaches. This tutorial explores core leadership styles and provides a framework for deciding when to apply each."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Directive (Autocratic) Leadership"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The directive style involves the leader making decisions unilaterally and communicating clear, explicit instructions. While often viewed negatively in modern culture, it is highly effective in specific contexts."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When to apply: During critical production outages, high-stakes security incidents, or when onboarding junior engineers who need explicit guardrails."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When to avoid: Creative architectural brainstorming sessions, or with senior, autonomous engineering teams where it stifles innovation."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Transformational Leadership"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transformational leaders focus on inspiring and motivating teams by connecting daily tasks to a larger vision. They encourage out-of-the-box thinking and personal growth."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When to apply: During major technology migrations, company pivots, or when team morale and retention are low."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When to avoid: Strict, compliance-heavy environments where rapid, process-driven execution is required over innovation."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Servant Leadership"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Servant leaders prioritize the growth, well-being, and empowerment of their team members. Their primary goal is to remove blockers and enable others to succeed."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When to apply: When managing mature, highly skilled software engineering teams that require autonomy and removal of organizational friction."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When to avoid: Crisis situations requiring immediate, top-down command and control."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Situational Leadership Framework"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The key to modern technical leadership is situational awareness. You must assess the competence and commitment of your team before selecting a style."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"function determineLeadershipStyle(teamMaturity, taskUrgency) {
    if (taskUrgency === 'CRITICAL') {
        return 'Directive';
    } else if (teamMaturity === 'HIGH') {
        return 'Servant';
    } else {
        return 'Transformational/Coaching';
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By dynamically shifting between these core styles, engineering managers and tech leads can foster high-performing, resilient, and motivated teams."}]
